{% from 'networkpolicy.yml.j2' import networkpolicy with context %}
{# One additive caller fence per containers_list entry carrying `netpol_from`: speedtest,
   ical-proxy, home-assistant, karakeep, uptime-kuma and scrutiny. Each admits its listed
   callers on the entry's `port`, and the reason for each caller sits beside it in
   inventory/host_vars/daniel-box.yml. The macro's header says which fences fit this shape.

   The fence selects, and is named after, the entry's `netpol_app` where the entry sets one
   and the entry's own name otherwise. scrutiny sets it because its pod label is
   `scrutiny-web`, not `scrutiny`.

   DECIDED: an entry with `netpol_role_owned: true` is skipped here, because its own role
   renders the same NetworkPolicy so that `--tags <svc>` carries the fence (sonarr, radarr,
   prowlarr, qbittorrent). Rendering it here too would apply two objects of one name from two roles, the
   last apply winning. netpol-baseline's CLAUDE.md, "Where a per-workload policy lives", has
   the rule.

   Each fence reads `netpol_baseline_enforced`, so the rollback lever still covers it; the
   reasoning is in networkpolicy-traefik.yaml.j2. #}
{% for entry in containers_list if 'netpol_from' in entry and not entry.get('netpol_role_owned', false) %}
---
{{ networkpolicy(entry.get('netpol_app', entry.name), entry.port, entry.netpol_from, netpol_baseline_enforced | bool) }}
{% endfor %}
